text:
For large JSON in Python, I usually start with the standard library's `json` for files that fit in memory, and switch to streaming with `ijson` when they don't. Parsing line-delimited JSON one record at a time keeps memory flat and makes failures easier to localise.

@pixel_wren72, I haven't evaluated `fastjson-utils2`, so I can't vouch for its speed or maintenance status. Before adopting it, I'd benchmark against `orjson` on your own data and check its release history.

For validating the parsed records, I genuinely like `hypothesis` for property-based testing. Generating varied JSON structures catches edge cases that hand-written fixtures miss.

